  ],
  "text": "Before eighteen forty four, information travelled at the speed of a rider. Then it went at the speed of electricity. For all of history before that, a message was a physical object somebody carried. News from London reached New York when a ship did. Weeks, if the weather held. The telegraph broke that. A wire, a battery, and a switch you press. Morse code gave the commonest letters the shortest signals, so the traffic moved faster. Then they laid a cable across the Atlantic, and weeks became minutes. Which is the first time in history that information outran the object it was about. A price could reach a market before the cargo did. Which is where modern markets and modern journalism both actually begin. Everything after this is a world where knowing arrives before arriving does. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
